 the three lines we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}=S,\n\\]\n\nso every required lattice point lies on at least one of the three lines.\n\nThe three lines are distinct, and each is either horizontal or vertical; none has slope \\(-1\\). Consequently none of them is sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists. \u220e"
