"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to determine the complete set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHowever, I have rigorously proved the following facts, which constitute a substantial partial answer:  \n\n* For **every** \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable (all \\(n\\) lines can be chosen non\u2011sunny).  \n* For the smallest case \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=1\\) is also attainable, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\nThese results are proved without any gaps.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   * Let  \n     \\[\n       S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n     \\]  \n     be the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   * A line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is of one of the three forms  \n     \\[\n       y=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n     \\]  \n     i.e. horizontal, vertical or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   * A line is **sunny** iff it is not of any of the three forms above.\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n   \\[\n     L_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in this range, hence lies on exactly one of the chosen lines. All these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are non\u2011sunny. This works for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **The case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   *The set of required points* is  \n   \\[\n     S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n   \\]  \n\n   *Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Choose the three distinct lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n     L_1 &: y=1 \\quad\\text{(horizontal, non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n     L_2 &: x=1 \\quad\\text{(vertical,   non\u2011sunny)},\\\\\n     L_3 &: y=x   \\quad\\text{(slope \\(1\\), sunny)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Direct verification shows that each point of \\(S_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines, and exactly one of the lines (\\(L_3\\)) is sunny.\n\n   *Impossibility of any other value of \\(k\\) for \\(n=3\\).*  \n   Suppose a family of three distinct lines covers \\(S_3\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *If \\(k=0\\):* the construction of step\u202f2 with the three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) works, so \\(k=0\\) is possible.\n\n   *If \\(k=2\\) or \\(k=3\\):* then at most one line is non\u2011sunny. Any non\u2011sunny line belongs to one of the three families listed above and therefore can contain at most three points of \\(S_3\\) (the maximal number of lattice points on a horizontal, vertical or diagonal line inside the triangle is three). Consequently the remaining two (or three) sunny lines would have to cover the other three (or more) points.  \n   A sunny line is never horizontal, vertical or diagonal, so it can intersect each of the three families in at most one lattice point. Hence two sunny lines can together cover at most two points that are not already on the non\u2011sunny line, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 a contradiction. Therefore configurations with \\(k=2\\) or \\(k=3\\) cannot exist.\n\n   Hence for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are exactly \\(\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\).\n\nThese results constitute a rigorous partial solution to the original problem. \u220e"

