e \\(k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(k\\) that the set  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncan be covered by **exactly** \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and no non\u2011sunny line.\n\n*Base \\(k=0\\).*  The set is empty; the statement is vacuous.\n\n*Base \\(k=1\\).*  The single point of \\(S_1\\) is \\((1,1)\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=x\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and contains \\((1,1)\\).  Thus \\(S_1\\) is covered by one sunny line.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the claim holds for \\(k-1\\) (\\(k\\ge 2\\)).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_k:\\;y = x + (k-1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\), therefore it is sunny.  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) the point  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;i+k-1)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nlies on \\(\\ell_k\\).  Because \\(i+(i+k-1)=2i+k-1\\le k+1\\) exactly when \\(i\\le k\\), all points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) belong to \\(S_k\\).  \nThus \\(\\ell_k\\) contains precisely the \\(k\\) points of the right\u2011most diagonal of the triangle \\(S_k\\).\n\nRemove these points from \\(S_k\\).  The remaining set is  \n\n\\[\nS_k'=\\{(a,b)\\in S_k\\mid a> b-(k-1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine new coordinates \\(a''=a-1,\\;b''=b-(k-1)\\).  \nThen \\(a'',b''\\ge 1\\) and \\(a''+b''\\le k\\); consequently \\(S_k'\\) is isomorphic to \\(S_{k-1}\\).\n\nBy the induction hypothesis, \\(S_{k-1}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k-1\\) distinct sunny lines (all of slope \\(1\\) but with different intercepts).  Translating those lines back to the original coordinates yields \\(k-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(S_k'\\).\n\nTogether with \\(\\ell_k\\) we obtain a covering of the whole set \\(S_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and no non\u2011sunny line.  This completes the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Assembly for the original problem  \n\nRecall th