on, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines exists.  \n   The obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines) shows that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.  \n   Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently it can be written as one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\ny=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) we exhibit three distinct lines covering these points and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  \\[\n  L_1:x+y=2,\\qquad L_2:x+y=3,\\qquad L_3:x+y=4 .\n  \\]\n  All three are of type (4) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_3\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\), hence lies on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  \\[\n  L_1:y=1,\\qquad L_2:x=1,\\qquad L_3:y=x+1 .\n  \\]\n  The first two are horizontal and vertical (non\u2011sunny); the third has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny.  \n  The horizontal line covers \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); the vertical line covers \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); the sunny line covers \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  The remaining point \\((3,1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) after a translation of coordinates, but it is already covered by the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_3\\) is on at least one of the three lines.\