y the induction hypothesis.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{L_{n+1}\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,k}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines (the added line is different from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because its slope is \\(-1\\) whereas every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) either has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or a value different from \\(-1\\)).  \nIt covers every point of \\(S_{n+1}\\) (points of \\(S_n\\) by the induction hypothesis, the new points by \\(L_{n+1}\\)).  \nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{\\,n+1,k}\\) is exactly the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), namely \\(k\\), because \\(L_{n+1}\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\nThus, assuming the statement for \\(n\\), we have proved it for \\(n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe base case \\(n=3\\) has been settled in Section\u202f1, and the inductive step of Section\u202f2 shows that the property propagates from \\(n\\) to \\(n+1\\).  By mathematical induction, for **every** integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe obvious inequality \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines) shows that no value \\(k>n\\) can occur.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
