            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\nn=3 &: \\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\ge 4 &: \\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nIn other words, the only impossible value is \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently its equation is one of  \n\n   \\[\n   y=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **The diagonal family.**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   are all non‑sunny and each point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **The case \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Keep the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines with the largest constants,\n   \\[\n   D_s\\;(s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The only uncovered point is \\((1,1)\\).  \n   The sunny line \\(L:\\;y=x\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.  \n   Thus \\(k=1\\) is possible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Replace every non‑sunny line by a diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) (this never reduces the set of covered points, because a diagonal line contains at least as many points of \\(S_n\\) as any horizontal or vertical line).  \n   After discarding the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines with the largest constants we are left with the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two of the three points is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are forbidden.  \n   Consequently each sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points, so at least three sunny lines are needed – a contradiction.  \n   Hence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n5. **Covering the “small triangle’’ with \\(k\\ge 3\\) sunny lines.**  \n\n   *5.1  The uncovered region.*  \n   Keep the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines with the largest constants,\n   \\[\n   D_s\\;(s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   All points of \\(S_n\\) with sum \\(\\ge k+2\\) are already covered.  \n   The remaining points form the right‑angled integer triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   which contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_k\\mid a+b=s\\}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   be the “diagonal’’ of sum \\(s\\); \\(|\\Delta_s|=s-1\\le k\\).\n\n   *5.2  A transversal of the diagonals.*  \n   Consider the bipartite graph \\(G\\) whose left part is the set of diagonals\n   \\(\\{\\Delta_2,\\dots ,\\Delta_{k+1}\\}\\) and whose right part is the set of rows\n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  \n   An edge joins \\(\\Delta_s\\) to a row \\(r\\) iff the point \\((r,s-r)\\) belongs to \\(\\Delta_s\\) (i.e. \\(1\\le r\\le s-1\\)).  \n   For a fixed row \\(r\\) the incident diagonals are exactly \\(\\Delta_{r+1},\\Delta_{r+2},\\dots ,\\Delta_{k+1}\\); therefore the degree of every vertex on each side equals \\(k\\).  \n\n   By **Kőnig’s line‑colouring theorem** (equivalently, Hall’s marriage theorem) a bipartite graph of maximum degree \\(\\Delta\\) possesses a *perfect matching* when the two parts have the same cardinality \\(\\Delta\\).  \n   Hence there exists a set of edges  \n\n   \\[\n   M=\\{(\\Delta_{s_i},r_i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   such that each diagonal \\(\\Delta_{s_i}\\) and each row \\(r_i\\) occurs exactly once.  \n   The corresponding points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(r_i,\\,s_i-r_i)\\in T_k\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k)\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct, no two share the same row (first coordinate) and no two share the same column (second coordinate).\n\n   *5.3  Construction of the sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\) we define the line \\(L_i\\) to be the unique line passing through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i\\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q_i=(r_i+1,\\,s_i-r_i-1),\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   where the second point \\(Q_i\\) also belongs to the same diagonal \\(\\Delta_{s_i}\\) (it is the neighbour of \\(P_i\\) along that diagonal).  \n   Because the two points lie on the same diagonal, the segment \\(P_iQ_i\\) is **not** horizontal, vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\); consequently the line \\(L_i\\) has a slope different from \\(0,\\,-1,\\infty\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n\n   Moreover, the two points of each line belong to distinct rows and distinct columns, so no two of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) coincide; thus we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   *5.4  Verification of the covering.*  \n   Every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on some diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\).  \n   By the definition of the matching \\(M\\), each diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) contains exactly one of the points \\(P_i\\).  \n   The line \\(L_i\\) passes through **all** points of \\(\\Delta