nes with the largest constants,\n   \\[\n   D_s\\;(s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The only point of \\(S_n\\) not covered by (4) is \\((1,1)\\).  \n   The line\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=x\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   passes through \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore it is sunny.  \n   The family consisting of the \\(n-1\\) lines (4) together with \\(L\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line. Thus \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n\n   *Step 4.1 – Reduction to the “small triangle”.*  \n   Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Replace every non‑sunny line by a diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) with the same constant \\(s\\).  \n   This replacement never destroys any covered point, because a diagonal line \\(x+y=s\\) contains **at least** as many points of \\(S_n\\) as any horizontal line \\(y=c\\) or vertical line \\(x=c\\) having the same constant (the diagonal contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\), while a horizontal line contains only those with a fixed \\(b\\)).  \n   Consequently we may assume that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are precisely the diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   D_{s_1},D_{s_2},\\dots ,D_{s_{\\,n-2}}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   for distinct constants \\(s_i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n   *Step 4.2 – Which diagonals can be omitted?*  \n   There are \\(n\\) different diagonals in total; therefore exactly two of them are **not** present in (6).  \n   Let \\(s_{\\min}\\) be the smaller of the two omitted constants.  \n   If \\(s_{\\min}\\ge 4\\) then the diagonal \\(D_{s_{\\min}}\\) contains at least three points (its size is \\(s_{\\min}-1\\ge 3\\)).  \n   A sunny line can intersect a given diagonal in **at most one** point, because any two distinct points of the same diagonal determine the diagonal itself, whose slope is \\(-1\\) and is forbidden for a sunny line.  \n   Hence the two sunny lines together can cover at most two