\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). All are non‑sunny.  \n- Therefore any sunny line can contain at most one of the three points. Two sunny lines can cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This argument is correct; the impossibility of \\(k=2\\) is established.\n\n### 3. Base Cases for the Inductive Construction (Section 3)\n\n#### a) \\(k=0\\)\n\n> “the empty family of lines trivially covers the empty set \\(T_0\\).”\n\n**Verification:** \\(T_0=\\{(a,b):a+b\\le1\\}\\) contains no positive integer points, so the empty family indeed covers it.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n#### b) \\(k=1\\)\n\n> “the single sunny line \\(y=x\\) contains the only point of \\(T_1\\), namely \\((1,1)\\).”\n\n**Verification:** \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line \\(y=x\\) (slope 1) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and is sunny.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n#### c) \\(k=3\\)\n\n> “the three sunny lines  \n> \\(L_1:y=x,\\;L_2:\\) through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)),  \n> \\(L_3:\\) through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\))  \n> cover the six points of \\(T_3\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(3,1),(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n- \\(L_1\\) covers \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \n- \\(L_2\\) covers \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\).  \n- \\(L_3\\) covers \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n- Slopes \\(-\\tfrac12\\) and \\(-2\\) are neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); all three lines are sunny.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The base case \\(k=3\\) is correctly constructed.\n\n### 4. Inductive Step (Section 3)\n\n> “Assume that for some \\(k\\ge3\\) (with \\(k\\neq2\\)) we have already constructed \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covering \\(T_k\\).  \n> We construct \\(k+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+1}\\).  \n> The new outer diagonal of \\(T_{k+1}\\) consists of the points \\(O_i=(i,\\;k+2-i)\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k+1)\\).  \n> For each \\(i\\) we choose a point \\(P_i\\in T_k\\) already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) as follows:  \n> \\[\n> P_i=\n> \\begin{cases}\n> (i,i), &\\text{if }i\\text{ is odd and }2i\\le k+1,\\\\[2pt]\n> (i-1,i+1), &\\text{if }i\\text{ is even and }2i\\le k+1,\\\\[2pt]\n> (1,\\,k+2-i), &\\text{otherwise}.\n> \\end{cases}\n> \\tag{12}\n> \\]  \n> Define \\(L_i\\) to be the unique line through \\(O_i\\) and \\(P_i\\).  \n> A straightforward computation of the slope shows that for every admissible \\(i\\) the slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the points \\(O_i\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k+1}\\) are distinct.  \n> Now replace, in the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), the (unique) line that contains \\(P_i\\) by the new line \\(L_i\\).  \n> Because \\(P_i\\) belongs to both the old line and the new line, all points of \\(T_k\\) remain covered.  \n> Moreover each new line \\(L_i\\) also contains the fresh point \\(O_i\\).  \n> Hence after performing this replacement for all \\(i=1,\\dots ,k+1\\) we obtain a family of exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines that covers every point of \\(T_{k+1}=T_k\\cup\\{O_1,\\dots ,O_{k+1}\\}\\).”\n\n#### 4.1 Correctness of the definition of \\(O_i\\) and \\(P_i\\)\n\n- The points \\(O_i\\) are indeed the integer points on the new diagonal \\(a+b=k+2\\); this is correct.  \n- For each case in (12) the solution checks that the coordinates are positive integers and satisfy \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), so \\(P_i\\in T_k\\). This part is valid.\n\n#### 4.2 Claim about the slopes of the lines \\(L_i\\)\n\n**Critical Error:**  \n- If \\(i\\) is odd and \\(2i\\le k+1\\), then \\(P_i=(i,i)\\) and \\(O_i=(i,k+2-i)\\). Both points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(i\\); the line \\(L_i\\) is vertical, i.e. slope \\(\\infty\\). This contradicts the claim that the slope is never \\(\\infty\\).  \n- If \\(i\\) is even and \\(2i\\le k+1\\), then \\(P_i=(i-1,i+1)\\) and \\(O_i=(i,k+2-i)\\). The slope is  \n  \\[\n  m_i=\\frac{k+2-i-(i+1)}{i-(i-1)}=k+1-2i.\n  \\]  \n  For many admissible \\(i\\) this value equals \\(0\\) (when \\(2i=k+1\\)) or \\(-1\\) (when \\(2i=k+2\\)). Hence the line can be horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\), both non‑sunny.  \n- Consequently the statement “the slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)” is false for a large class of indices \\(i\\). The construction does **not** guarantee that the new lines are sunny.\n\nBecause the inductive step relies on the new lines being sunny, this error breaks the logical chain of the proof. All subsequent conclusions that depend on the existence of \\(k+1\\) sunny lines are invalid.\n\n#### 4.3 Uniqueness of the line containing \\(P_i\\) in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\)\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \n- The solution assumes that each point \\(P_i\\) lies on a *unique* line of the current family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). No argument is provided that the covering \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) has the property that each point of \\(T_k\\) belongs to exactly one line. In general a covering by lines may have points lying on several lines. Without uniqueness the instruction “replace the (unique) line that contains \\(P_i\\)” is ambiguous.  \n\n#### 4.4 Possibility of replacing the same line multiple times\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \n- Even if each \\(P_i\\) lies on at least one line, the construction does not ensure that the lines containing the different \\(P_i\\)’s are distinct. If two distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\) happen to have \\(P_i\\) and \\(P_j\\) on the same line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), the algorithm would attempt to replace that line twice, which is impossible after the first replacement. The solution does not address this issue.\n\n#### 4.5 Distinctness of the new lines \\(L_i\\)\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \n- The argument “the points \\(O_i\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct” is insufficient. Two different pairs \\((O_i,P_i)\\) and \\((O_j,P_j)\\) could still define the same line if the four points are collinear. The solution does not rule out this possibility (although the slope error already shows many of the \\(L_i\\) are non‑sunny, which is a stronger problem).\n\n#### 4.6 Overall validity of the inductive construction\n\nBecause of the critical error concerning the slopes (Section 4.2) and the multiple justification gaps (Sections 4.3–4.5), the inductive step does **not** establish the existence of \\(k+1\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+1}\\). Hence the claim that the sub‑problem is solvable for every \\(k\\ge3\\) is unproved.\n\n### 5. Completion of the Original Problem (Section 4)\n\n> “If \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) … If \\(k\\ge3\\) … By the result of Section 3 we can cover the remaining set \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines … If \\(k=2\\) … no configuration exists.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The conclusions for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are correctly based on the earlier sections (which are valid for those values).  \n- The conclusion for \\(k\\ge3\\) depends on the inductive construction of Section 3, which, as shown, is flawed. Therefore the statement that a configuration exists for every \\(k\\ge3\\) is not justified.\n\n### 6. Final Answer\n\n> “Consequently the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The answer follows from the (incorrect) claim that the sub‑problem is solvable for all \\(k\\ge3\\). Since the proof of solvability for those \\(k\\) is invalid, the answer is not established.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution correctly handles the reduction and the impossibility of \\(k=2\\), and it provides valid base cases for \\(k=0,1,3\\). However, the inductive construction intended to cover all larger \\(k\\) contains a **critical error** (the new lines are not guaranteed to be sunny) and several **justification gaps** (uniqueness of lines containing the chosen points, possible multiple replacements, and distinctness of the new lines). Consequently the proof that all \\(k\\ge3\\) are attainable is unsound, and the final answer is not rigorously established. The solution is therefore **invalid**."
